  "minimum_safe_summary": "This empirical study investigated how the aging of materials influences user perception and emotional responses. Through experiments with 25 participants evaluating new and aged polymeric products and samples, the study found that emotional and sensorial variations are proportionally related to material aging. Specifically, disgust increased and satisfaction decreased with product aging, and photometric properties like transparency and saturation also varied. While sight was found to dominate emotional evaluation, textile properties were better at maintaining emotional bonds over time compared to superficial visual aspects. A key limitation was the small participant panel size, which restricts the generalizability of quantitative conclusions.",
